<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Punakha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punakha">Punakha</a> was signed in January 1910 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> doubled Bhutan's annual subsidy and promised not to interfere in its internal affairs, while Bhutan agreed to be guided by Britain in its external relations. The treaty secured British India's northern frontier against Chinese influence, and its terms were largely carried over into Bhutan's 1949 treaty with independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-punakha.jpg" alt="Treaty of Punakha" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Technopilgrim at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PunakhaDzong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between 1864 and 1865 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> fought and lost the five-month Duar War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Under the Treaty of Sinchula, signed on 11 November 1865, it ceded territory in the Assam Duars and Bengal Duars and the eighty-three square kilometre district of Dewangiri in the south-east, in return for an annual subsidy of 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. By the turn of the century, changing circumstances in the region raised the question of a new agreement, and in December 1907 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, who had consolidated power as Penlop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trongsa">Trongsa</a>, was unanimously elected monarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed at Punakha Dzong on 8 January 1910 by Charles Alfred Bell, Political Officer in Sikkim, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-gyalpo">Druk Gyalpo</a>, and ratified by the Viceroy and Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in Council at Fort William on 24 March 1910. It was not a stand-alone document but an amendment of Articles IV and VIII of the Treaty of Sinchula, whose other terms it kept.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> raised the annual allowance from 50,000 to 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> with effect from 10 January 1910 and undertook not to interfere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>'s internal administration, while Bhutan agreed to be guided by British advice in its external relations. Disputes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of Sikkim and Cooch Behar were to be referred to British arbitration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Agreements made at the same time shared the profits of elephant hunting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s favour, assigned land at Motithang and a hill station between Chukha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> to the British, and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> part of British Kalimpong, which later became Bhutan House, owned by the Dorji family. The treaty set a precedent for foreign conduct of Bhutan's external relations and for foreign subsidy to its government.</p><p>Once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was independent, Sonam Topgay Dorji led a Bhutanese delegation that met Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> on 23 April 1948, and the talks ended in the Indo-Bhutan Treaty of 8 August 1949, under which India returned the land around Deothang. In February 2007 King Jigme Khesar Namgyal Wangchuck signed a new treaty of friendship with India, replacing that of 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Punakha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Punakha</b> (1910) then made Bhutan a de facto protectorate of the British Empire, whose protection guarded it from Tibet and Qing China; the friendship treaty with India of 1949 took over that role, and the border with China was closed in 1960 after an influx…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Military</a></li></ul></section>
